WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
User-facing error messages and diagnostic logs serve different audiences.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:11.560
A user needs to know whether a request failed, whether they can retry, or whether they should contact support.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:19.420
Operators need protected diagnostic detail that helps identify the failing component, dependency, route, or workflow.

4
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.320
Mixing those audiences creates either poor support or excessive disclosure.

5
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.000
Exceptions should be logged safely.

6
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:36.860
A log entry can include exception type, safe message category, ExampleRequestId, component name, dependency name, and sanitized context.

7
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:48.880
It should avoid secrets, tokens, connection strings, private records, full request bodies, and unnecessary internal details that are not needed by the intended log reader.

8
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.440
Development diagnostics should not become production behavior.

9
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:57.980
Detailed local errors and broad framework logs help developers learn what went wrong.

10
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.680
Production should use safe user responses, protected logs, and correlation IDs.

11
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:10.500
If more detail is temporarily needed in production, it should be scoped, approved, and removed after use.

12
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.380
Log levels should match expectedness and impact.

13
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.560
Expected validation failures may belong at a lower level or may be aggregated as metrics.

14
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.980
Handled dependency failures may need warning-level visibility.

15
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:30.600
Unexpected exceptions that affect user actions or data integrity deserve stronger attention.

16
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.140
Consistent levels make monitoring and alerting more reliable.

17
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.380
Swallowed exceptions hide important signals.

18
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:45.400
A catch block that absorbs failure without logging, returning a clear outcome, or updating state can make the system appear healthy when it is not.

19
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.940
Defensive error handling should preserve useful context while avoiding sensitive data exposure.

20
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:55.380
Repeated high-volume exception logs can create noise and cost.

21
00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:03.160
If one dependency failure generates thousands of identical entries, teams may need aggregation, rate control, or alert tuning.

22
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:10.300
The goal is to make failures observable, not to flood responders with repeated copies of the same information.
